    "q-44331": {
      "dom_id": "step-q44331",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following ordered pairs is **NOT** a solution to $y=3x+4?$\n\nI. $(4,16)$\n\nII. $(0,4)$\n\nIII. $(3,12)$",
      "worked_solution": "To check whether each point is a solution to the equation, we substitute it into the equation and see if it results in a true statement.\n\nFirst, we substitute $(4,16)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x+4 \\\\ (16) & =3(4)+4 \\\\ 16 & =12+4 \\\\ 16 & =16\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a true statement, so $(4,16)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nNext, we substitute $(0,4)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x+4 \\\\ (4) & =3(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is again a true statement, so $(0,4)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nFinally, we substitute $(3,12)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x+4 \\\\ (12) & =3(3)+4 \\\\ 12 & =9+4 \\\\ 12 & =13\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a false statement, so $(3,12)$ is *not* a solution to the equation.\n\nSo, out of the three ordered pairs given, the only pair that is *not* a solution to the equation is III $(3,12).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
